A||N||6||bHooper||car died on the expressway, what do you think?||||5||
Z||000000||bHooper||01-17-2001||09:15 AM||||hey guys, my car died on the expressway today.  it lost power as i took off in the passing lane, and when i pulled over on the left side of the road... it died all together.<P>yesterday it ran fine, the only thing different is i changed the coil, last night, to a new accel one.  the car turns over, it just doesnt get any spark.<P>so what do you think?  ignition module?  how hard are these to replace... can it ealily be done on the side of the highway?<P>thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||5
Z||000001||Songman||01-17-2001||09:28 AM||||Sounds like the module to me.. or maybe the new coil died...What model is the car? <P>You could change a module on the side of the highway.... but I would rather not if there is any other way...<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||01-17-2001||09:51 AM||||It coil be the module or the pick-up coil. When my car did that it was the pick-up coil.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||bHooper||01-17-2001||10:03 AM||||fortunately, i wasn't to far from Bob Englert's house, and he was kind enough to pick me up.   <B>A BIG THANKS TO BOB!!!</B><P>he took me home, to grab another car and my tools... i made sure to grab the old coil and tools... i forgot to get the duct tape, so i don't know if i can do it or not.<P>what is necessary to change out the control module?<P>thanks!<P>hoop||||reg||2
Z||000004||william_thorniii||01-17-2001||10:23 AM||||hoop,<P>The first thing you should do is pull that Accel coil.  Most products they make are marginal at best.  If that does not fix your problems, THEN look at coils, etc.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000005||Raydar||01-17-2001||12:23 PM||||Hoop, the module is held in place in the bottom of the distrib by two machine screws. Think they're 5 mm, but not sure. You don't need to pull the distrib to replace the module. Just pull off the cap and rotor.  Everything else plugs in. Use the GM module, if you can. I've heard aftermarket ones are not as reliable. Be sure to use the heat-sink compound (just a thin coating between the module and base plate) if you replace it. <BR>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||01-17-2001||12:30 PM||||the car is fixed.  i bought a new module for $25 from Advance Auto (GP Sorenson), checked the old coil, still no fire, then pulled the cap (it is a 5 mm socket), and replaced the control module.<P>the car fired right up!<P>the distributer and module looked a little discusting, a fair amount of oxidation.  i think it's time for a rebuild or new one.<P>and of course, i left the perfectly running jeep to drive the car that died on me this morning  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks to everyone that has helped... especially for Bob and Lynn, for giving me a ride and allowing me to abandon my Jeep, in front of their house, so that I could drive my fiero!<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
